1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,000
This is Klorn, an AI email attention firewall at app.klorn.ai.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,500
Klorn connects to a user's Gmail and Google Calendar and classifies every
message into five attention lanes — PUSH, MEETING, QUEUE, INFO, and SILENT —

3
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:25,000
so users are only interrupted by mail that needs them. I'll sign in with
Google and show how each requested scope powers a user-facing feature.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Signing in with Google asks only for basic profile information — Klorn
follows incremental authorization, so no mail access is requested at login.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Gmail and Calendar permissions are requested separately, when the user
connects their inbox. Here is that consent screen for our production OAuth
client — note the client ID in the address bar.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:01:00,000
It lists Gmail read, send, and modify access, plus Calendar events and
read-only Calendar access. The user sees exactly what is requested and
consents before any data is accessed.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Immediately after connecting, Klorn uses the Gmail read-only scope to sync
recent messages and classify each one into an attention tier.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Message bodies are read to produce these per-message summaries and the
daily briefing. This classification is the core of the product.

9
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Mail content is used only for this classification and summarization; it is
never used for advertising and never read by humans.

10
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:03,000
When the user acts on a triaged message, Klorn uses the gmail.modify scope
to reflect that decision in the real mailbox —

11
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:14,000
applying tier labels, marking read, archiving, or moving a message to Trash
after explicit approval. Deletes always go to Gmail's reversible Trash.

12
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Here in Gmail you can see the same message with Klorn's changes applied.

13
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Replying uses the gmail.send scope, gated behind explicit user approval.
The user reviews and can edit the draft.

14
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:52,000
On approval, Klorn records a receipt of the exact approved content and sends
precisely those bytes — the system refuses to send anything that doesn't
match what was approved.

15
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Here is the message in the Gmail Sent folder.

16
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Calendar access powers meeting context. With the read-only Calendar scope,
Klorn checks free/busy across all of the user's calendars to flag conflicts
like this.

17
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:32,000
With the Calendar events scope, the user can approve turning an email into
a calendar event without leaving the triage flow.

18
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Here is the event in Google Calendar.

19
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Klorn also ships a desktop client. It authenticates through the system
browser with the same OAuth client and the same identity-only sign-in — no
embedded webviews and no additional permissions.

20
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:15,000
The desktop app is another view of the same account; all Google data access
still happens on our backend, shown earlier.

21
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Users stay in control: they can disconnect Google or delete their Klorn
account at any time, which purges all Google-derived data from our systems.

22
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Access can also be revoked from the user's Google Account permissions page.

23
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
All Google user data is stored encrypted and used only for the features
shown in this video, in accordance with the Limited Use policy. Thank you.
